OpenChronology server for Arch Linux
====================================

Install
-------

From the AUR, with an AUR helper or by hand:

    git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/openchronology-server.git
    cd openchronology-server && makepkg -si

It builds och-convert and the pgmp extension for this system's PostgreSQL, and installs
the server, the `openchronology` system user (and `openchronology-logs`, the log
importers') and its services. As Arch packages do, it
leaves PostgreSQL and the database to you; finish with:

    sudo openchronology-setup

which:

  - initializes PostgreSQL in /var/lib/postgres/data if it is not yet, and starts and
    enables it;
  - writes /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env, with a new AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY;
  - creates the `openchronology` PostgreSQL role and database, with the pgmp extension;
  - migrates the database (openchronology-migrate) and starts openchronology.service,
    which listens on 127.0.0.1:5173.

It is safe to run again. The server runs on Arch's Node.js (`nodejs`, or an LTS package
that provides it).

PostgreSQL major upgrades: pgmp is built for the PostgreSQL this package was built with.
After pacman installs a new PostgreSQL major version, upgrade the cluster as the Arch
wiki describes (pg_upgrade), and rebuild this package so pgmp matches it.

Configure
---------

Edit /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env, then `sudo systemctl restart openchronology`:

  - APP_ORIGIN: the public HTTPS origin users reach, without a trailing slash. The server
    refuses a plain http:// origin other than localhost; on a trusted private network
    without a proxy, set ALLOW_INSECURE_ORIGIN=1 and HOST=0.0.0.0 to serve
    http://<name>:5173 directly (and open the port in your firewall). If the website reports "Server
    misconfigured", the reason is also in `journalctl -u openchronology`.
  - RESEND_API_KEY and AUTH_EMAIL_FROM for account email, then
    `sudo systemctl enable --now openchronology-mail`.
  - ADMIN_USERNAME, ADMIN_EMAIL and ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD for the first administrator,
    created by `sudo openchronology-migrate`. The password is used once; to sign in with it
    again (or with a new one written there), run `sudo openchronology-reset-admin-password`.
  - Sign-in providers, human verification, quotas: see the comments in the file and
    authentication.md, administration.md in this directory.

Back up AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY apart from the database: without it, enrolled authenticator
secrets cannot be decrypted. Do not change it once accounts exist.

Serve it publicly through an HTTPS reverse proxy. With Caddy:

    timescale.example {
        reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5173
    }

Caddy 2.5 and later replace an incoming
X-Forwarded-For with the client address, so set TRUST_PROXY=1 (with another proxy, only if
it does the same; see README.md). Proxies should allow request bodies of at least 32 MiB
and streaming responses (live updates).

The syslog receiver (syslog.md) is configured in /etc/openchronology/syslog.env with routes
in /etc/openchronology/syslog-routes.json; then
`sudo systemctl enable --now openchronology-syslog`.

The GitHub receiver (github.md) mirrors issues and pull requests into timelines from
GitHub's webhooks, on 127.0.0.1:5580 behind your HTTPS reverse proxy. It is configured in
/etc/openchronology/github.env (the API key and the webhook's secret) with routes in
/etc/openchronology/github-routes.json; then
`sudo systemctl enable --now openchronology-github`.

The journald follower (journald.md) streams this machine's systemd journal, or another
machine's over SSH, into timelines. Configure it in /etc/openchronology/journald.env (the timeline or
routes, and which units); then `sudo systemctl enable --now openchronology-journald`.

The log importers run as their own system user, openchronology-logs, which cannot read
openchronology.env or log in to the database (#501); the journald follower has the
systemd-journal group as well. syslog.env, github.env and journald.env are
root:openchronology-logs, mode 0640, and so must be the files they name (routes, the
journald SSH key and known hosts): `sudo chown root:openchronology-logs FILE && sudo chmod
0640 FILE`. See syslog.md and journald.md, "Its own account".

`och-import-logs` imports historic log files (log-import.md), with OCH_API_URL and
OCH_API_KEY from the environment or syslog.env: `och-import-logs --help`.
`och-import-github` imports a repository's existing issues and pull requests (github.md),
with OCH_API_URL, OCH_API_KEY and GITHUB_TOKEN from the environment or github.env:
`och-import-github --help`.

Upgrade
-------

Installing a newer package (from the AUR, `makepkg -si` again, or your AUR helper's upgrade)
stops the server and leaves the database and configuration as they are. It then lists:

  1. Settings this version's template has that /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env
     lacks. Compare with:
         diff -u /etc/openchronology/openchronology.env \
                 /usr/share/openchronology/openchronology.env.example
  2. A backup:  sudo -u postgres pg_dump openchronology > openchronology.sql
  3. sudo openchronology-migrate, which migrates the database and starts the server, and
     each of the mail worker (openchronology-mail) and the log importers
     (openchronology-syslog, openchronology-github, openchronology-journald) that is
     enabled. Disabled ones stay stopped.

openchronology-migrate is safe to run more than once. `--no-restart` migrates only and starts
nothing.

Upgrading from a version before #501 moves syslog.env, journald.env and the files they name
(SYSLOG_ROUTES, JOURNALD_ROUTES, JOURNALD_SSH_KEY, JOURNALD_KNOWN_HOSTS) from the
openchronology user to openchronology-logs, keeping their modes: only regular files with one
link, reached through no symbolic link, in folders only root can change. It lists what it
moved and what it left for you to change.

Remove
------

`sudo pacman -R openchronology-server` stops and removes the server. The configuration
(/etc/openchronology, including AUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY), the database and the system users
remain; delete them with:

    sudo rm -rf /etc/openchronology /var/lib/openchronology /var/cache/openchronology
    sudo -u postgres dropdb openchronology
    sudo -u postgres dropuser openchronology
    sudo userdel openchronology
    sudo userdel openchronology-logs

Layout
------

  /usr/lib/openchronology            the application and och-convert (node/bin/node is
                                     this system's /usr/bin/node)
  /usr/bin/openchronology-setup      first-time setup (run it once after installing)
  /usr/bin/openchronology-migrate    database migration
  /usr/bin/openchronology-reset-admin-password   the administrator's password, from the configuration
  /usr/bin/och-import-logs           log file import
  /usr/bin/och-import-github         GitHub import
  /etc/openchronology                configuration (created by openchronology-setup)
  /usr/share/openchronology          configuration templates of the installed version
  /usr/lib/systemd/system            openchronology, openchronology-mail, openchronology-syslog,
                                     openchronology-github, openchronology-journald
  /usr/lib/postgresql, /usr/share/postgresql/extension   pgmp for this system's PostgreSQL
